Verdict: bug found

Line 25: `if(inside==1) ck(pthread_cond_wait(&cv,&m));`

The entry section treats a wakeup as capacity permission even though no occupant may have left.

| Step | Thread A | Thread B | Shared state |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | read inside -> 1 under m |  | elevator full |
| 2 | cond_wait releases m and waits |  | occupant B still inside |
| 3 | wait returns spuriously |  | inside still 1 |
| 4 | ++inside |  | capacity violation |

Fix:
```diff
--- prog.c
+++ prog.c (fixed)
@@ -22,7 +22,7 @@
 static int inside=1;
 static void *a(void *p) {
     (void)p; ck(pthread_mutex_lock(&m));
-    if(inside==1) ck(pthread_cond_wait(&cv,&m));
+    while(inside==1) ck(pthread_cond_wait(&cv,&m));
     ++inside; assert(inside<=1); --inside; ck(pthread_mutex_unlock(&m)); return NULL;
 }
 static void *b(void *p) {
```

while keeps admission tied to inside rather than a notification. B can acquire m during the wait, leave and signal actual space.

Verdict: bug found

Line 24: `ck(pthread_mutex_unlock(&outbox)); return NULL;`

The exit section names a different mutex. It neither owns outbox nor releases inbox; the invalid unlock has no portable default-type semantics.

| Step | Thread A | Thread B | Shared state |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | lock(inbox) succeeds |  | inbox=A; outbox free |
| 2 | increment removed |  | A still owns inbox |
| 3 | unlock(outbox) |  | wrong object; inbox not released |
| 4 |  | lock(inbox) attempts | inbox remains unavailable; prior unlock invalid |

Fix:
```diff
--- prog.c
+++ prog.c (fixed)
@@ -21,7 +21,7 @@
 static int removed;
 static void *a(void *p) {
     (void)p; ck(pthread_mutex_lock(&inbox)); ++removed;
-    ck(pthread_mutex_unlock(&outbox)); return NULL;
+    ck(pthread_mutex_unlock(&inbox)); return NULL;
 }
 static void *b(void *p) { return a(p); }
 int main(void) {
```

Unlocking inbox matches the actual acquisition and lets the next remover enter. The unrelated outbox remains untouched until safe destruction.

Verdict: bug found

Line 24: `printf("checksum=%d\n",checksum);`

A join only orders actions that follow its successful return. Printing first can observe unfinished data and has no ordering against the worker write.

| Step | Thread A | Thread B | Shared state |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | worker not yet finished |  | checksum initially 0 |
| 2 |  | main reads checksum | no completion edge yet |
| 3 | worker writes checksum |  | can conflict with main’s read |
| 4 |  | main joins worker | ordering came after the read |

Fix:
```diff
--- prog.c
+++ prog.c (fixed)
@@ -21,7 +21,7 @@
 static void *worker(void *p) { (void)p; checksum=21; return NULL; }
 int main(void) {
     pthread_t t; ck(pthread_create(&t,NULL,worker,NULL));
+    ck(pthread_join(t,NULL));
     printf("checksum=%d\n",checksum);
-    ck(pthread_join(t,NULL));
     return 0;
 }
```

Move join before the observation, so completion and the worker’s write happen before main reads the result.

Verdict: bug found

Line 24: `if(depth>0) visit(depth-1);`

Each recursive call repeats normal-mutex acquisition while its ancestor still owns m.

| Step | Thread A | Thread B | Shared state |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | visit(2) locks m |  | m=A |
| 2 | visit(1) tries to lock m |  | recursive call waits for its own caller |

Fix:
```diff
--- prog.c
+++ prog.c (fixed)
@@ -20,11 +20,10 @@
 static pthread_mutex_t m;
 static int visited;
 static void visit(int depth) {
-    ck(pthread_mutex_lock(&m));
     if(depth>0) visit(depth-1);
-    ++visited; ck(pthread_mutex_unlock(&m));
+    ++visited;
 }
-static void *a(void *p) { (void)p; visit(2); return NULL; }
+static void *a(void *p) { (void)p; ck(pthread_mutex_lock(&m)); visit(2); ck(pthread_mutex_unlock(&m)); return NULL; }
 static void *b(void *p) { return a(p); }
 int main(void) {
     pthread_t x,y;
```

One outer entry protects the whole walk; the recursive helper performs no lock acquisition and the wrapper executes one exit. It remains safe because all callers use the wrapper.
